Give every WebUI page the new tab page's palette.

One accent, one outline, one pair of text colours in each scheme, for Settings,
History, Downloads, Extensions and every bubble built out of cr_elements. The
values are the ones chrome://aerium-newtab already draws with.

This is the shared half of what aerium-settings-style.patch did for one page.
That patch stated the palette in settings_vars.css, which only Settings imports;
this states it in cr_shared_vars.css, which every WebUI page does, and the
settings patch keeps only the part that is genuinely about Settings.

TOKENS, NOT RULES, for the reason that made the settings version work: a
stylesheet cannot reach into a shadow root, but a custom property inherits
through one, and every cr_elements component takes its colours from the same
small set of names. Restating the names restyles all of them, and a component
upstream adds next milestone arrives already themed.

The selector is html:root rather than html. cr_shared_vars.css itself has an
html[webui-refresh-2026] block that a bare html would lose to on specificity;
:root matches its weight, and this block is last in the file, so it wins on
order instead.

Both --cr-* and --color-* are stated, and that is not redundancy. The controls
resolve --color-* first and fall back to the cr palette only when it is absent -
cr_toggle.css reads --color-toggle-button-track-on and cr_button.css reads
--color-button-background-prominent before anything else - so an accent given
only to the cr tokens never reaches a toggle or a button. The colour pipeline
declares the --color-* names on the document at runtime; this sheet is adopted,
and adopted sheets cascade after linked ones, so it lands on top.

COLOURS ONLY, DELIBERATELY. The card shape - the transparent fill and hairline
ring the design puts round a group - stays in the settings patch, applied to the
one page whose card markup has been read. history_item.css draws its list as a
single segmented card, with #background split across is-card-start and
is-card-end and a box-shadow drawn deliberately past the edge to join the rows;
a ring token there would draw a line between every row. Downloads is the same
shape of risk. Those pages get the palette and keep their own geometry.

The dark ground is #060B16 and the raised surface #0E1834, which are the values
aerium-brand-surfaces already gives ui::kColorSysBase and ui::kColorSysSurface,
so a WebUI page and the window frame around it are the same two colours rather
than four that nearly match. Light is #F2F7FD and white, the new tab page's own.

Nothing else in the series touches cr_elements - checked by grepping patches/
for the directory, which returns only this patch's own prose.

Verified against the real 152.0.7977.82 source: the file was fetched at that
tag, edited, the patch generated from the pair, and re-applied to a clean copy -
it lands with no fuzz and reproduces the file byte for byte. cr_elements is
built with build_webui, which runs the same stylelint that rejected the first
version of the settings patch, so the same rules were checked by hand first: no
collapsible six-digit hex, no duplicate custom property in any block (seven
blocks, none), a trailing newline, and both new rules preceded by a comment,
which rule-empty-line-before ignores.

--- a/ui/webui/resources/cr_elements/cr_shared_vars.css
+++ b/ui/webui/resources/cr_elements/cr_shared_vars.css
@@ -377,3 +377,96 @@
     --cr-animations-disabled: 1;
   }
 }
+
+/* --- Aerium: one palette for every WebUI page.
+ *
+ * These are the values chrome://aerium-newtab draws with, restated as the
+ * tokens the cr_elements components already read, so Settings, History,
+ * Downloads, Extensions and every bubble built out of these components share
+ * one accent, one outline and one pair of text colours in each scheme.
+ *
+ * Tokens rather than rules, because a stylesheet cannot reach into a shadow
+ * root but a custom property inherits through one. It also means a component
+ * added upstream next milestone arrives already themed.
+ *
+ * html:root, not html, on purpose: the html[webui-refresh-2026] block above
+ * would win against a bare html on specificity. :root matches its weight, and
+ * this block is last in the file, so it wins on order instead.
+ *
+ * Both --cr-* and --color-* are stated. The controls resolve --color-* first
+ * and fall back to the cr palette only when it is absent - cr_toggle.css reads
+ * --color-toggle-button-track-on and cr_button.css reads
+ * --color-button-background-prominent before anything else - so an accent
+ * given only to the cr tokens never reaches a toggle or a button. The colour
+ * pipeline declares the --color-* names on the document; this sheet is
+ * adopted, and adopted sheets cascade after linked ones.
+ *
+ * Colours only. Card shape - the transparent fill and hairline ring the design
+ * puts round a group - is left to aerium-settings-style.patch, which applies it
+ * to the one page whose card markup has actually been read. History draws its
+ * items as one segmented card and would grow a ring between every row. */
+html:root {
+  --aerium-bg: #F2F7FD;
+  --aerium-surface: #FFF;
+  --aerium-outline: #D8E6F5;
+  --aerium-outline-dim: #E1ECF9;
+  --aerium-accent: #2C6BAE;
+  --aerium-on-accent: #FFF;
+  --aerium-text: #0B2138;
+  --aerium-label: #4A5D7A;
+
+  --md-background-color: var(--aerium-bg);
+  --color-webui-page-background: var(--aerium-bg);
+
+  --cr-card-background-color: var(--aerium-surface);
+  --color-webui-card-background: var(--aerium-surface);
+  --cr-menu-background-color: var(--aerium-surface);
+  --cr-menu-background-focus-color: var(--aerium-outline-dim);
+  --cr-dialog-background-color: var(--aerium-surface);
+  --color-webui-dialog-background: var(--aerium-surface);
+
+  --cr-separator-color: var(--aerium-outline-dim);
+  --cr-scrollable-border-color: var(--aerium-outline);
+  --cr-focused-item-color: var(--aerium-outline-dim);
+  --cr-hairline: 1px solid var(--aerium-outline-dim);
+
+  --cr-primary-text-color: var(--aerium-text);
+  --cr-secondary-text-color: var(--aerium-label);
+  --cr-title-text-color: var(--aerium-label);
+  --cr-form-field-label-color: var(--aerium-label);
+  --cr-link-color: var(--aerium-accent);
+  --cr-checked-color: var(--aerium-accent);
+  --cr-focus-outline-color: var(--aerium-accent);
+
+  --color-primary-foreground: var(--aerium-text);
+  --color-secondary-foreground: var(--aerium-label);
+  --color-link-foreground-default: var(--aerium-accent);
+  --color-checkbox-foreground-checked: var(--aerium-accent);
+  --color-toggle-button-track-on: var(--aerium-accent);
+  --color-toggle-button-thumb-on: var(--aerium-on-accent);
+  --color-toggle-button-track-off: transparent;
+  --color-toggle-button-thumb-off: var(--aerium-label);
+  --color-button-border: var(--aerium-outline);
+  --color-button-foreground: var(--aerium-accent);
+  --color-button-background-prominent: var(--aerium-accent);
+  --color-button-foreground-prominent: var(--aerium-on-accent);
+  --color-button-background-tonal: var(--aerium-outline-dim);
+  --color-button-foreground-tonal: var(--aerium-text);
+}
+
+/* The dark ground is the value aerium-brand-surfaces gives ui::kColorSysBase
+ * and the raised surface is its kColorSysSurface, so a WebUI page and the
+ * window frame around it are the same two colours rather than four that nearly
+ * match. */
+@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
+  html:root {
+    --aerium-bg: #060B16;
+    --aerium-surface: #0E1834;
+    --aerium-outline: #22376E;
+    --aerium-outline-dim: #1B2A57;
+    --aerium-accent: #7FC4E4;
+    --aerium-on-accent: #06283D;
+    --aerium-text: #D8E6F5;
+    --aerium-label: #8FA3C4;
+  }
+}
